  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nMain forecast concern today will be strong northwesterly winds this \nafternoon. Some lingering cold air advection into the region will \ntrend wind speeds slightly to the higher end, mainly focused over \nnortheastern South Dakota and western Minnesota. Wind gusts in that \narea will reach as high as 30 to 35 miles per hour, with widespread \ngusts around 25 miles per hour across the remainder of the forecast \narea. Winds will die down beginning this evening due to both he \ndiurnal cycle as well as a high pressure center pushing into the \nNorthern Plains. While winds are strongest, there will be some \nlocalized areas of High Grassland Fire Danger over far northeastern \nSouth Dakota and into western Minnesota. \n\nA cooler airmass will remain over the forecast area into the middle \nof next week. Through Monday 850mb temperatures will generally range \nin the -5 to -10 degrees Celsius range, which is roughly between the \n10th and 25th percentiles for this time or year. As a result, highs \nwill be in the 30s both today and Monday. A bit of a warmup comes \nMonday night, and by the middle of the week 850mb temperatures will \nsettle in near freezing (close to the median for this time of year). \nHigh will increase into the 40s through at least the middle of the \nweek as a result. \n\nThe main precipitation chances being monitored in the forecast \nperiod come Tuesday night into Wednesday. A jet streak aloft on the \nback side of a shortwave will be the main driver for any possible \ndevelopment, but chances remain quite slim regardless. There has \nbeen a decrease over the latest forecast cycle, and now there is \nonly a 10-20% chance to see a hundredth of an inch Tuesday through \nearly Wednesday, mainly over northeastern south Dakota and western \nMinnesota. Any precipitation will likely fall as snow, but little to \nno accumulation is expected and impacts will be minimal.",
